---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "kaged protein powder",
      "kaged whey protein isolate",
      "kaged protein review",
      "is kaged protein third party tested",
      "kaged micropure whey isolate"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Kaged Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Kaged's whey isolate carries an Informed Sport certification and a digestive enzyme. We check the label, the testing, the sweeteners and what it really costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-kaged">Who Makes Kaged</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in Kaged Whey Isolate</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#testing">Is Kaged Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#compare">How Kaged Compares</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip Kaged</a>
      <a href="#using">Getting the Most From It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Kaged Whey Protein Isolate is a lean, well-tested whey: 25 g of protein in about 110 to 130 calories with roughly 1 g of sugar, plus the ProHydrolase enzyme, and Kaged says it is Informed Sport certified, which means batches are screened for banned substances. At about $57 to $60 for a 3 lb tub of roughly 40 servings it costs about $1.45 to $1.50 per 25 g of protein, which is fair for a certified isolate. The main catch is the sweetener: it uses sucralose alongside stevia, so buyers avoiding artificial sweeteners should look elsewhere.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Kaged Protein: The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kaged Whey Protein Isolate is a good certified isolate at a fair price. It gives 25 g of protein per scoop with very little sugar, fat or lactose, it adds the ProHydrolase enzyme, and Kaged says it is Informed Sport certified, so batches are tested for substances banned in sport. At about $1.45 to $1.50 per 25 g in a 3 lb tub, it sits in the middle of the market for isolates: cheaper than most premium brands, a little dearer than the biggest mass-market tubs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Kaged Whey Isolate by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">About 1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sugar per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">About $1.47</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g (3 lb tub)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Informed Sport</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Certification (brand)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The case against it is narrow but real. Kaged sweetens the isolate with stevia and sucralose, and reviewers, including one published taste review, describe it as very sweet. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, or you want a lightly sweet shake, Kaged is not the isolate for you, however good its testing is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict is based on the label, the brand's certification claims and retailer prices, not on tasting it ourselves. Buy Kaged if you are drug-tested or simply want an isolate that an outside lab has screened, and you are fine with sucralose. Choose BPN's isolate if you want certification without sucralose, and Dymatize ISO100 if you want a certified isolate in more flavors and bigger tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the full field of isolates, our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> rankings compare Kaged with every isolate we track on label, testing and price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-kaged" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Kaged?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kaged started as Kaged Muscle, the supplement brand associated with Welsh bodybuilder and fitness author Kris Gethin, who built a large audience through online training programs. Supplement trade coverage from 2018 refers to it as Kris Gethin's Kaged Muscle, and the brand has since shortened its name to Kaged and refreshed its packaging. Its isolate was sold as Micropure Whey Protein Isolate before a 2022 rebrand renamed it Kaged Whey Protein Isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            That history makes Kaged an influencer brand in origin, but one that has leaned on testing rather than personality for trust. Its pitch has long been that its products are tested by Informed Sport, the banned-substance program run by LGC, which is aimed at athletes who face drug testing. Kaged's own product page says the isolate is third-party tested for hundreds of banned substances for Informed Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kaged's range runs well beyond protein: pre-workouts, creatine, stimulant-free products and recovery formulas. In protein, the main products are the Whey Protein Isolate, a casein called Kaged Kasein, and Re-Kaged, a whey-based post-workout recovery formula with added ingredients. This review focuses on the isolate, the product most people mean when they search for Kaged protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            You will find Kaged in more stores than most influencer brands. We found the isolate at Target and H-E-B, and it has been listed on Bodybuilding.com. Wide retail distribution is not a quality signal in itself, but it does mean prices are easy to compare, which helps buyers. For other influencer-led brands we have reviewed, see <a href="/bare-performance-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Bare Performance Nutrition</a> and <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Kaged Whey Protein Isolate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The label is short. Kaged lists whey protein isolate as the protein, the ProHydrolase enzyme blend, natural flavor, salt and a gum blend, and the sweeteners stevia and sucralose, with ingredients varying slightly by flavor. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop of about 34 g gives 25 g of protein, about 110 to 130 calories, 2 to 4 g of carbohydrate, 0 to 1 g of fat and around 1 g of sugar, depending on flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twenty-five grams of protein in a 34 g scoop is about 74 percent protein by weight, lower than the 85 to 90 percent a pure, unflavored isolate can reach, because flavor, salt, gums and sweetener take up the rest. That is normal for a flavored isolate and still leaner than most whey blends. Kaged describes the protein as gluten-free, soy-free and non-GMO, with no artificial flavors or colors, though sucralose is an artificial sweetener.
          </p>

          <p>
            ProHydrolase is an enzyme blend sold to supplement makers on the claim that it helps break whey down into smaller peptides and amino acids. A small 2020 study using 250 mg of ProHydrolase with 26 g of whey reported higher blood amino acid levels than whey alone. Kaged does not disclose the dose in its isolate on the pages we read, and the research is limited and partly industry-funded, so treat the enzyme as a possible bonus for comfort rather than a proven muscle-building advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener is the decision point. Most flavors use both stevia and sucralose. Sucralose is approved as safe by the FDA and other regulators, and most mainstream wheys use it, but many buyers prefer to avoid it, and the combination is what makes the shake so sweet. If that is you, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide lists certified options sweetened only with stevia, monk fruit or nothing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE LABEL VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A lean, short-label isolate with an enzyme and a sweet finish.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Kaged's label is clean apart from sucralose. The protein per scoop, low sugar and low lactose all suit a daily shake; the sweetness is the part to try before you commit to a big tub.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Kaged Whey Isolate and the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is Kaged's flagship protein. The other two are the alternatives we would point a buyer to depending on what matters most: a cheaper certified isolate, and a certified isolate without sucralose. Prices are from retailer listings we found in November 2026 and vary by flavor, store and sale; label figures are per the brand or label at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Kaged Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Kaged states that its Whey Protein Isolate is Informed Sport certified, and Informed Sport's public database has listed Kaged's isolate under both its old Micropure name and as a whey protein isolate, with certification dates going back to 2018. Informed Sport tests a sample of every batch before release for a long list of substances banned in sport, and certifies the manufacturing site. It is one of the two main programs, with NSF Certified for Sport, that drug-tested athletes rely on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport is a banned-substance program. It does not certify that a product is free of heavy metals or that its protein content is exactly as labeled, though manufacturers in the program are audited. Kaged did not publish heavy metal results for its isolate on the pages we read. Whey isolates generally test lower for metals than plant proteins and chocolate flavors, so a vanilla Kaged isolate is a lower-risk choice by category, but that is a general point rather than a measurement of Kaged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport itself notes that it does not sell products and that brand pages are outside its control, which is why the database, not a logo on a website, is the place to check. If you are tested in sport, search Informed Sport's site for Kaged Whey Protein Isolate, confirm your flavor and batch number, and keep the tub's lot number in case you ever need it. That is the standard advice for any certified product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with the brands that dominate trust searches, Kaged sits in a strong position: Informed Sport places it alongside Ascent and Momentous, and above clean-label brands that rely only on their own testing claims. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">best NSF certified protein powder</a> guide covers the other main certification, and our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide explains what each program does and does not test.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does Kaged Protein Cost per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In a 3 lb tub, Kaged Whey Protein Isolate costs about $1.43 to $1.50 per 25 g of protein at the prices we found. A 3 lb tub is about 1,361 g of powder; at about 34 g a scoop, that is roughly 40 servings, or about 1,000 g of protein. At Target's $59.99 listing that is about $1.50 per 25 g, and at H-E-B's $57.18 it is about $1.43.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Source and size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price seen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein in tub</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Target, 3 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$59.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,000 g (about 40 x 25 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.50</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">H-E-B, 48 oz (3 lb)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$57.18</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1,000 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.43</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smaller tub, about 1.8 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by retailer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 625 g (about 25 x 25 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually higher per gram</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Servings per tub vary slightly by flavor, because scoop weight changes with the flavoring. Reviews have reported 41 to 44 servings in a 3 lb tub for some flavors and fewer for others, and one listing described a 2.79 lb tub with 37 servings. Check the panel on your flavor; the cost per 25 g will move by a few cents either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            The smaller tub of about 25 servings is the trial size and usually costs more per gram, with listed prices we saw ranging widely between retailers. If you already know you like the flavor, the 3 lb tub is the better buy. Kaged and its retailers also run frequent sales, so a patient buyer can often beat the figures above.
          </p>

          <p>
            In context, $1.45 to $1.50 per 25 g is a fair price for a certified isolate. It is cheaper than <a href="/bare-performance-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">BPN's isolate</a> at list price and in the same range as many isolates in big tubs. It is roughly double what Costco's uncertified Kirkland whey costs, as our <a href="/how-much-is-costco-protein-powder/">Costco protein powder price</a> breakdown shows, which is the gap you pay for an isolate with an Informed Sport certificate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Does Kaged Compare With Other Isolates?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize ISO100</a>, the two are close. Both give about 25 g of protein with about 1 g of sugar, both use sucralose, and both are certified (ISO100 carries the Informed Choice logo, Kaged Informed Sport). ISO100 is hydrolyzed and comes in more flavors and bigger tubs; Kaged adds an enzyme. Choose on flavor and price per gram on the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against BPN's Grass-Fed Whey Isolate, Kaged is cheaper and BPN is sucralose-free. BPN uses stevia (Reb M) only and grass-fed Irish whey and is NSF Certified for Sport, but it costs about $2.60 per 25 g at list price, or about $1.82 on sale. If sucralose is a dealbreaker, BPN is the certified answer; if not, Kaged saves money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure</a>, Kaged's advantage is certification. Isopure Zero Carb is leaner still, with essentially no carbohydrate, but we have not confirmed a sport certification for it, so a drug-tested athlete should choose Kaged. Against <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs</a>, whose whey isolate is Informed Choice certified, publishes certificates of analysis and avoids sucralose, Transparent Labs matches Kaged on testing and beats it on sweetener, usually at a higher price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against a mainstream whey blend such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard, Kaged costs a little more per gram but gives you an isolate rather than a blend, which means less lactose, less fat and a thinner, lighter shake. For many people the blend is fine; for a sensitive stomach or a lean diet, the isolate is the upgrade. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Kaged Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Kaged if you avoid sucralose or dislike very sweet shakes. Every Kaged isolate flavor we found uses sucralose with stevia, and taste reviews single out the sweetness. A certified isolate sweetened with stevia alone, such as BPN's, or an unflavored isolate you sweeten yourself, will suit you better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you have a milk allergy. Whey isolate is still a dairy protein, and Kaged's label lists milk. Lactose intolerance is different: isolates contain very little lactose and many lactose-sensitive people tolerate them, especially starting with half a scoop. Anyone with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and people on medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement; this is not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if price per gram is your only goal. At about $1.47 per 25 g, Kaged is fair for a certified isolate but costs about twice as much as the cheapest big-bag wheys. If you are not drug-tested and a whey blend agrees with you, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> guide will save you money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you want a plant protein. Kaged's protein range is dairy-based, so vegans and dairy-free buyers need another brand. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide covers certified plant options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="using" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Getting the Most From Kaged Whey Isolate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use more liquid than the label suggests if you find it too sweet. Kaged recommends one scoop in 5 to 8 ounces of cold water, which makes a concentrated, very sweet shake. Ten to twelve ounces, or a mix of water and milk, softens the sweetness without changing the protein you get.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use it after training or between meals. An isolate digests quickly, and 25 g of whey supplies roughly 2.5 g of leucine, enough to switch on muscle protein synthesis for most adults. If you want a slower protein before bed, Kaged's casein or a <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein protein powder</a> suits that job better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blend it into food if you like a sweet base. The sweetness that bothers some people in a shake works well stirred into plain Greek yogurt, overnight oats or a smoothie with unsweetened fruit, where it does the job of added sugar. Isolates can turn rubbery when baked, so add it to batters in modest amounts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the lot number if you are tested. If you compete in a drug-tested sport, photograph the tub's lot number and check it against Informed Sport's database when you buy. That record is your evidence that you took reasonable care, which is the whole point of buying a certified product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming certified means sucralose-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kaged's Informed Sport certification is about banned substances, not sweeteners. Most Kaged isolate flavors use sucralose with stevia. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, check the label, or choose a stevia-only certified isolate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the logo instead of the database</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A certification logo on a website is not proof for your tub. Search Informed Sport's database for Kaged Whey Protein Isolate, confirm your flavor and batch, and keep the lot number if you are drug-tested.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The roughly 25-serving tub usually costs more per gram than the 3 lb tub. Once you know you like a flavor, buy the 3 lb size, ideally on sale, to keep the cost near $1.45 per 25 g.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the enzyme to build more muscle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                ProHydrolase may help digestion and amino acid absorption, but the research is small and Kaged does not disclose its dose. The 25 g of whey isolate does the muscle-building work; treat the enzyme as a possible comfort bonus.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing it too strong</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One scoop in 5 ounces of water makes a very sweet shake. If the sweetness puts you off, add more water or milk before deciding you dislike the product; the protein dose stays the same.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Kaged protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Kaged states that its Whey Protein Isolate is Informed Sport certified, meaning samples of each batch are tested for substances banned in sport before release, and Informed Sport's database has listed it. Check your flavor and batch in the database if you are drug-tested.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Kaged protein healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label it is a lean isolate: 25 g of protein, about 1 g of sugar, little fat, with whey isolate, an enzyme, flavor, salt, gums and sweeteners. It is gluten-free and soy-free, per the brand. The one ingredient clean-label buyers object to is sucralose.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Kaged whey isolate contain sucralose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, in the flavors we found. Kaged sweetens its isolate with stevia and sucralose, and taste reviews describe it as very sweet. If you avoid sucralose, BPN's isolate uses stevia only and is NSF Certified for Sport.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Kaged whey isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  25 g per scoop of about 34 g, per the label at the time of writing, with about 110 to 130 calories and around 1 g of sugar depending on flavor. A 3 lb tub holds roughly 40 servings, varying a little by flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Kaged protein contain lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Very little. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most lactose, so many lactose-sensitive people tolerate it, but it is not labeled lactose-free and it is still a milk protein. People with a milk allergy should avoid it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Kaged protein flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Kaged does not rank its flavors and we have not tasted them. Flavors have included Vanilla, Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Cinnamon French Toast and S'mores, with stock varying. Because buyers describe the isolate as very sweet, vanilla or chocolate are the most versatile for mixing into food.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What happened to Kaged Micropure whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It was renamed. Kaged Muscle's Micropure Whey Protein Isolate became Kaged Whey Protein Isolate in a 2022 rebrand, and the brand itself shortened its name from Kaged Muscle to Kaged. The core formula, whey isolate with ProHydrolase, stayed the same, per trade coverage of the change.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

